var tafsirData_48 = {
  "1": "“Indeed, We have granted you a clear triumph ˹O Prophet˺” opens Surah al-Fath with an announcement whose occasion the commentators establish with care, for the whole chapter turns upon it. The surah was revealed as the Prophet returned from al-Hudaybiyyah, where he had gone with his companions intending to perform the lesser pilgrimage and had been turned back by Quraysh, and where a treaty had been concluded on terms that many of the Muslims at first received as a setback. Ibn Kathir relates the background: in the sixth year of the Hijrah the Prophet saw in a vision that he was entering the Sacred Mosque, and he set out with about fourteen hundred companions, unarmed except for the swords of travelers, seeking not war but pilgrimage. Quraysh barred the way, and after long negotiation a truce was written — no fighting for ten years, the Muslims to return that year without entering Mecca, and to come the following year for the pilgrimage. The companions, and Umar most of all, were grieved by terms they saw as humiliating, and it was in that atmosphere of disappointment that God revealed, “Indeed, We have granted you a clear triumph,” or “inna fatahna laka fathan mubeena.”\n\nThe commentators explain what the “clear triumph” was. Al-Tabari records that the verse was understood by the Prophet's companions, once its meaning became plain, as referring to the treaty of Hudaybiyyah itself, and the Prophet is reported to have said that no greater victory had been granted to him than that treaty. The triumph was not a conquest of territory but a conquest of peace, for the truce recognized the Muslims as a power to be negotiated with, opened the way for the message to spread, and within two years brought more people into Islam than had entered it in all the years before. Al-Razi observes the depth of the lesson: what the believers took for a defeat was in fact the greatest victory, and the verse teaches that the judgment of God on events is not the judgment of men, for the event that grieves the heart today may be the door of triumph tomorrow.\n\nAl-Qurtubi notes that the surah opens with the past tense — “We have granted” — for a future whose certainty God already knew, and the believer is taught that the promises of God are so sure that they may be spoken of as already fulfilled. The word “mubeen” — “clear” — distinguishes this triumph from every ambiguous gain, for it was a triumph whose fruits would become plain to all. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read events with the eye of faith rather than the eye of immediate feeling, to trust that the wisdom of God lies beneath the surface of what appears, and to remember that the treaty the companions mourned became the triumph the Quran proclaimed.",
  "2": "“so that Allah may forgive you for your past and future shortcomings, perfect His favour upon you, guide you along the Straight Path,” states the purpose of the triumph, and the verse has occupied the commentators because of the word rendered here as “shortcomings.” “So that Allah may forgive you for your past and future shortcomings,” or “li-yaghfira laka Allahu ma taqaddama min dhanbika wa ma ta'akhkhar.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse addresses the Prophet, and that the forgiveness spoken of is the forgiveness of what the term “dhanb” means in the case of the Prophet — not sins in the sense of willful disobedience, for the prophets are protected from that, but the lesser lapses of human judgment and the choices that fell short of the best, the matters for which the Prophet himself sought forgiveness in his prayers and his repentance. Al-Tabari records the same understanding, and the commentators agree that the verse is among the honors of the Prophet, declaring that God has forgiven him his past and his future, so that no one may hold against him any failing in the delivery of the message.\n\nThe verse then continues with the other purposes: “perfect His favour upon you, guide you along the Straight Path,” and the next verse adds the help. Al-Razi observes that the purposes are arranged in a beautiful order — forgiveness first, for the servant's standing with his Lord is the foundation; then the perfecting of favor, for the completion of blessing follows the completion of pardon; then guidance upon the Straight Path, for the forgiven and favored servant is led and kept. The believer is taught that the triumphs God grants are not ends in themselves but means to these greater ends, and that a victory which does not bring one nearer to forgiveness, favor, and guidance is no victory at all.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson for the community. The Prophet was promised forgiveness, perfected favor, and guidance, and the community that follows him is promised the same in proportion to its following, and the believer is taught to seek from every success the three things the verse names, to ask that his victories increase his nearness rather than his pride. The verse also teaches the believer the posture of the Prophet, who, though promised forgiveness, did not cease to seek it, for the promise of forgiveness is an invitation to gratitude, not a license to negligence. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to receive every blessing as a means to a higher end, and to measure his days by whether they are bringing him closer to the forgiveness, favor, and guidance that the verse names.",
  "3": "“and so that Allah will help you tremendously.” completes the purposes of the triumph with the promise of help, and the verse is as short as it is comprehensive. “And so that Allah will help you tremendously,” or “wa yansuraka Allahu nasran 'azeezan.” The word “'azeez” — “mighty,” “tremendous” — describes a help that cannot be withstood, and the commentators explain that the help promised here is the help of God against every enemy, the help that would carry the Prophet and his community through the years that followed the treaty, through the conquest of Khaybar, through the entering of Mecca, and through the spreading of the faith across the land. Ibn Kathir notes that the promise was fulfilled in the most visible way, for the treaty that the companions had mourned was followed within two years by the conquest of Mecca itself, and the help of God carried the message to victory after victory.\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse teaches the believer the order of the divine purposes — forgiveness, favor, guidance, and then help — for the help of God is not given to exalt the servant but to complete the work of the message, and the help that is sought before the purification of the servant is a help that would corrupt him. The believer is taught to seek the help of God as the fruit of the forgiveness and favor and guidance that precede it, and to know that the help of God is mighty precisely because it is given in the service of the truth and not in the service of the self. Al-Razi notes that the word “yansuraka” — “He will help you” — is addressed to the Prophet, and through him to the community, for the help of God to His messenger is the help of God to all who stand with the messenger.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to distinguish between the help of God and the help of the world. The help of the world is measured in armies and wealth, and it can be taken away in a day, while the help of God is the help that parted the sea and broke the wind of 'Ad, and it cannot be measured or withstood. The believer is taught to attach his hope to the help that is “'azeez” — mighty — and to know that the treaty of Hudaybiyyah, which looked like weakness, was the beginning of the mightiest help. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to trust the promise, to work and wait, and to remember that the help of God comes in His time and His way, and that the event which seems a defeat may be the first step of the “nasr 'azeez.”",
  "4": "“He is the One Who sent down serenity upon the hearts of the believers so that they may increase even more in their faith. To Allah ˹alone˺ belong the forces of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.” explains the inner mechanism of the triumph, and the verse introduces the word that is the heart of the surah. “He is the One Who sent down serenity upon the hearts of the believers so that they may increase even more in their faith,” or “huwa alladhi anzala as-sakeenata fee quloobi al-mu'mineena li-yazdadoo eemanan ma'a eemanihim.” The word “sakeenah” — “serenity,” “tranquility” — describes a peace that descends from God into the heart, a stillness that calms the agitation and turns anxiety into trust, and the commentators note that it was this serenity that transformed the companions at Hudaybiyyah, who had come grieving and doubtful and were made still and certain. Ibn Kathir explains that the “sakeenah” is a gift from God, sent down upon the hearts at the moments of greatest trial, and that its effect is the increase of faith, for the heart that is made calm can see the wisdom it could not see in its agitation.\n\nAl-Tabari observes the precision of the verse in locating the triumph. The outward events — the treaty, the return, the disappointment — did not change, but the hearts changed, and the change of the hearts is the real victory, for the triumph of God is first a triumph within, a triumph of serenity over anxiety and of faith over doubt, before it is a triumph without. The verse then grounds the serenity in the divine power: “To Allah alone belong the forces of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise,” or “wa lillahi junoodu as-samawati wal-ardi wa kana Allahu 'aleeman hakeema.” Al-Razi notes that the mention of the forces of the heavens and the earth is the answer to every fear, for the believer who knows that the armies of all existence belong to God fears no army of men, and the serenity is the fruit of that knowledge.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that faith increases and decreases, and that the increase is the work of God through the gifts He sends down, and that the believer is taught to seek the “sakeenah” in his own trials, to ask God for the serenity that turns panic into trust. The verse also teaches that the serenity is sent down upon the hearts of the believers specifically, for it is the reward of the faith that was already there, and the increase of faith is given to those who have faith. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the order of the inner life — that calm precedes clarity, and clarity precedes strength — and that the one who would have the triumph without the serenity has put the end before the beginning.",
  "5": "“So He may admit believing men and women into Gardens under which rivers flow—to stay there forever—and absolve them of their sins. And that is a supreme achievement in the sight of Allah.” continues the chain of purposes with the reward of the believers, and the verse moves from the serenity of this world to the Garden of the next. “So He may admit believing men and women into Gardens under which rivers flow — to stay there forever — and absolve them of their sins,” or “li-yudkhila al-mu'mineena wal-mu'minati jannatin tajree min tahtiha al-anharu khalideena feeha wa yukaffira 'anhum sayyi'atihim.” The commentators note the deliberate inclusion of “believing men and women,” for the verse is an explicit statement that the reward of the Garden is not for men alone, and that the women who believed and pledged and sacrificed at Hudaybiyyah are partners in the promise, their faith weighed and their reward complete. Ibn Kathir explains that the absolving of sins — “yukaffir” — means that God covers and removes their sins, so that they enter the Garden with nothing held against them, and the verse then seals the promise with the words, “And that is a supreme achievement in the sight of Allah,” or “wa thalika huwa al-fawzu al-'atheem.”\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse places the worldly triumph in its true proportion. The treaty of Hudaybiyyah was a triumph of this world, but the verse teaches that the triumph of this world is only the means to the triumph of the next, and that the believer is not to be content with the one without seeking the other. The Garden, the forgiveness, and the “supreme achievement” are the real ends, and the events of Hudaybiyyah are their preface. Al-Razi notes the word “fawz” — “achievement,” “success” — which describes the winning of a prize, and the believer is taught to think of the Hereafter as the prize for which the trials of this world are the contest.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the mention of women is one of the honors Islam gave them, for the women who pledged at Hudaybiyyah are named among the believers whom God will admit to the Garden, and the verse stands as a permanent witness against those who would exclude women from the fullness of the divine promise. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to keep the end in view, to count no worldly gain as the final success, and to measure every event by whether it draws him toward the “fawz 'atheem,” the supreme achievement that is the Garden of God.",
  "6": "“Also ˹so that˺ He may punish hypocrite men and women and polytheistic men and women, who harbour evil thoughts of Allah. May ill-fate befall them! Allah is displeased with them. He has condemned them and prepared for them Hell. What an evil destination!” balances the promise to the believers with the warning to the others, for the surah does not speak of the triumph without speaking of its opposite. “Also so that He may punish hypocrite men and women and polytheistic men and women, who harbour evil thoughts of Allah,” or “wa yu'adhdhiba al-munafiqeena wal-munafiqati wal-mushrikeena wal-mushrikati ath-thanneena billahi thanna as-saw'.” The commentators explain that the triumph of Hudaybiyyah was a test that separated the sincere from the insincere, for the believers trusted the Prophet and found serenity, while the hypocrites and the idolaters harbored the “evil thought” that God would not help His messenger and that the believers would perish, and the verse declares that this thought was itself a sin for which they would be punished. Ibn Kathir notes that the “evil thought of Allah” is the thought that God will abandon His servants, the doubt that denies His power and His promise, and the verse names it as the root of their ruin.\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the thoughts of the heart, for the punishment is attached not only to deeds but to the “thann” — the thought, the assumption — that denies God, and the believer is taught to guard his thoughts as carefully as his limbs. The verse then pronounces the judgment: “May ill-fate befall them! Allah is displeased with them. He has condemned them and prepared for them Hell. What an evil destination!” or “'alayhim da'iratu as-saw'i wa ghadiba Allahu 'alayhim wa la'anahum wa a'adda lahum jahannama wa sa'at maseera.” Al-Razi notes the sequence of the judgment — the ill-fate, the displeasure, the condemnation, and the Fire — each a deeper degree of the same ruin, and the believer is taught the seriousness of the state to which doubt and hypocrisy lead.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the same event is a mercy to one and a punishment to another, for the treaty that brought serenity to the believers brought the exposure of the hypocrites, and the believer is taught that the trials of the community are the sieve by which God separates the sincere from the false. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to examine his own thoughts about God, to refuse the whisper that doubts His help and His promise, and to know that the thought of the heart is the seed from which the deeds of the limbs grow, and that the seed of trust bears the fruit of serenity while the seed of doubt bears the fruit of ruin.",
  "7": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belong the forces of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” returns to the theme of divine power, and the verse is a refrain that the surah repeats to fix a single truth in the heart. “To Allah alone belong the forces of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise,” or “wa lillahi junoodu as-samawati wal-ardi wa kana Allahu 'azeezan hakeema.” The commentators explain that the verse is placed here as the answer to the fear and the hope of the previous verses — the fear of the enemies and the hope of the believers — for the armies of the heavens and the earth, the angels and the winds and every force of creation, all belong to God and are at His command, and the believer who knows this fears no enemy and the enemy who forgets it is already defeated. Ibn Kathir notes that the repetition of the phrase in the surah is deliberate, for the lesson it teaches is the lesson the companions needed at Hudaybiyyah, when their own weakness was visible and the strength of their enemies seemed overwhelming.\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse teaches the believer to place the visible forces of this world in their true proportion. The armies of men are real, and the believer does not deny them, but they are only a small part of the forces that belong to God, and the One who commands the forces of the heavens and the earth can send a wind, a storm, or a host of angels to undo what the armies of men have built. The believer is taught to fear God and not the forces of the world, and to know that the help of God needs no army, for the forces of the heavens and the earth are His and He employs them as He wills. Al-Razi notes the pairing of the two names — “'Azeez,” the Almighty, and “Hakeem,” the All-Wise — for the power of God is never exercised without wisdom, and the believer is taught to trust both the power that can help and the wisdom that knows when and how to help.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer's confidence is not in numbers but in the Lord of numbers, and that the community which has God has the forces of all existence on its side, while the community which opposes God stands alone against all existence. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to repeat this truth in his own trials, to look from the armies of men to the Lord of the armies, and to find in the phrase “the forces of the heavens and the earth” the serenity that no earthly strength can give.",
  "8": "“Indeed, ˹O Prophet,˺ We have sent you as a witness, a deliverer of good news, and a warner,” turns from the power of God to the mission of the Prophet, and the verse describes his office in three titles that the commentators unfold one by one. “Indeed, O Prophet, We have sent you as a witness, a deliverer of good news, and a warner,” or “inna arsalnaka shahidan wa mubashshiran wa natheera.” The first title, “shahid” — “witness” — means that the Prophet bears witness to the truth of the message, testifying to the oneness of God and to the truth of what he has delivered, and that he will be a witness over his community on the Day of Judgment, testifying to who believed and who denied. Ibn Kathir explains that the witness is the one who has seen and known and will speak the truth of what he has seen, and the Prophet is the witness par excellence, for his witness is the witness of one who was sent by God and shown His signs.\n\nThe second title, “mubashshir” — “deliverer of good news” — describes the Prophet's relation to the believers, for he announces to them the good news of the Garden and the pleasure of God, and the third, “natheer” — “warner” — describes his relation to the heedless, for he warns them of the Fire and the punishment. Al-Tabari observes that the two titles together describe the wholeness of the prophetic mission, for the message of God is never only threat or only promise but the two together, the hope that draws and the fear that restrains, and the believer is taught to receive both, for the one who hears only the promise becomes complacent and the one who hears only the warning despairs. Al-Razi notes the order of the titles — witness first, then good news, then warning — for the witness establishes the truth, and upon the truth the good news and the warning are built.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to know his Prophet by these titles and to receive him in all three of his offices, and to understand that the community inherits something of each office, for the believer bears witness to the truth and carries good news and warning to others in proportion to his knowledge and his station. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught gratitude for the sending of the Prophet, for the witness, the good news, and the warning are three mercies that a people without a messenger would lack, and the surah places this verse among the purposes of the triumph to show that the triumph of the message is the triumph of these three offices over the falsehood that opposed them.",
  "9": "“so that you ˹believers˺ may have faith in Allah and His Messenger, support and honour him, and glorify Allah morning and evening.” states the purpose for which the Prophet was sent, and the verse turns from the titles of the Prophet to the duties of the believers. “So that you believers may have faith in Allah and His Messenger, support and honour him, and glorify Allah morning and evening,” or “li-tu'minoo billahi wa rasoolihi wa tu'azzizoohu wa tuwaqqiroohu wa tusabbihoohu bukratan wa aseela.” The commentators explain the four duties named. The first is faith in Allah and His Messenger, the foundation upon which everything else rests; the second is “tu'azzizoohu” — to support and strengthen the Prophet, to stand with him and give him the strength of the community; the third is “tuwaqqiroohu” — to honor and reverence him, to hold him in the esteem due to the messenger of God; and the fourth is “tusabbihoohu” — to glorify Allah morning and evening, in the constant remembrance that accompanies and completes the other duties. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse gathers the whole relation of the believer to God and His messenger into a single sentence.\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse teaches the order of the religious life — faith first, then the support and honor of the messenger, then the perpetual glorification of God — and that the glorification “morning and evening” describes not two moments only but the whole of the day, for the believer who glorifies God at the beginning and the end of the day has enclosed his day within the remembrance of God. Al-Razi notes the subtlety that the support and honor are given to the Prophet while the glorification is given to God alone, for the messenger is honored as a messenger but glorified is only the One who sent him, and the verse thus guards the boundary that the whole surah has been defending — the honor of the creature within the worship of the Creator.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson for the community of Hudaybiyyah and for every community after it. The triumph of the message required believers who would believe, support, honor, and glorify, and the verse taught the companions that their role in the triumph was not passive but active, for the victory of the Prophet was the victory of those who stood with him. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make his whole life the fulfillment of these four duties, to believe with certainty, to support with strength, to honor with reverence, and to glorify without ceasing, and to know that the community which keeps these duties is the community through which God grants His triumphs.",
  "10": "“Surely those who pledge allegiance to you ˹O Prophet˺ are actually pledging allegiance to Allah. Allah’s Hand is over theirs. Whoever breaks their pledge, it will only be to their own loss. And whoever fulfils their pledge to Allah, He will grant them a great reward.” interprets the pledge that the companions gave at Hudaybiyyah, and the verse is one of the most honored in the Quran, for it concerns the very pledge by which the believers bound themselves to the Prophet. “Surely those who pledge allegiance to you O Prophet are actually pledging allegiance to Allah. Allah's Hand is over theirs,” or “inna allatheena yubayi'oonaka innama yubayi'oona Allaha yadu Allahi fawqa aydeehim.” The commentators explain the occasion: when the rumor reached the Prophet at Hudaybiyyah that Uthman, whom he had sent as an envoy to Quraysh, had been killed, the Prophet sat beneath a tree and took an oath from the companions, each placing his hand in the Prophet's hand and pledging to stand and fight and not to flee, and this was the pledge that the Quran here lifts to its highest meaning. Ibn Kathir notes that the pledge was a pledge to the Prophet in form but to God in reality, for the messenger is the hand through whom the allegiance reaches the Lord, and the hand of God was over their hands as they pledged.\n\nThe verse then states the two possible outcomes of the pledge: “Whoever breaks their pledge, it will only be to their own loss. And whoever fulfils their pledge to Allah, He will grant them a great reward,” or “fa-man nakatha fa-innama yankuthu 'ala nafsihi wa man awfa bima 'ahada 'alayhu Allaha fa-sa-yu'teehi ajran 'atheema.” Al-Tabari observes the justice of the two clauses — the breaker harms only himself, and the fulfiller is rewarded greatly — and the believer is taught that his pledge to God is a contract whose terms are in his own interest, for God has no need of the pledge and the profit or loss of keeping or breaking it falls upon the one who pledged. Al-Razi notes the phrase “Allah's Hand is over theirs,” and explains it as the declaration of God's presence at the pledge, His blessing upon it, and His witness to it, for the pledge given under the tree was not a mere political act but an act of worship witnessed by God.\n\nAl-Qurtubi records that this verse and the tree beneath which the pledge was taken became so honored among the companions that they would later recount their presence there as their highest distinction, and the verse is the ground of that honor, for God Himself declares the pledge to be a pledge to Him. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the sanctity of the pledge and the seriousness of the word given, to keep his pledges to God and to men, and to know that the hand that pledges to the truth is a hand over which the Hand of God rests, and that the reward of the fulfilled pledge is the “ajr 'atheem” that no one can take away.",
  "11": "“The nomadic Arabs, who stayed behind” were groups around Medina who did not accompany the Prophet to Hudaybiyyah. They expected danger and preferred safety, then prepared an acceptable-sounding explanation after the believers returned. Their nomadic identity was not the sin; revelation judges the hypocrisy and choice of particular people.\n\n“We were preoccupied with our wealth and families” turns normal responsibilities into an excuse. Wealth and family have real rights, and genuine inability can excuse a duty. Here the claim hid a darker calculation. “Ask for forgiveness for us” sounded repentant, but “They say with their tongues what is not in their hearts.” True repentance agrees inwardly, admits wrong, and changes direction.\n\n“Who then can stand between you and Allah in any way, if He intends harm or benefit for you?” exposes their faulty idea of safety. Remaining home cannot block decreed harm, and difficult obedience cannot prevent benefit Allah grants. Precaution is lawful, but a false family excuse does not control destiny.\n\n“Allah is All-Aware of what you do” reaches beyond words to motive and conduct. Communities should not dismiss every family-related absence as hypocrisy; people judge credible circumstances fairly. Yet polished language cannot deceive Allah. Family care becomes worship when sincere and rightly prioritized, but becomes a shield for cowardice when invoked falsely. Honest people state limitations truthfully, seek forgiveness for real failure, and trust that obedience never removes them from Allah’s care.\n\nThe request “ask for forgiveness for us” also teaches that religious language can be used to avoid the very repentance it names. Asking a righteous person to pray is not wrong, but prayer cannot substitute for a heart that intends to continue deception. The Prophet’s supplication did not give people control over Allah’s judgment. Each person must turn sincerely. When words and heart agree, asking others to pray can accompany repentance; when they disagree, the request becomes another layer of performance known fully to the All-Aware.",
  "12": "“You thought that the Messenger and the believers would never return to their families again” reveals what those staying behind concealed. Their absence was not mere busyness. They predicted the Muslim party would be destroyed, so joining appeared foolish. The safe return exposed their calculation.\n\n“That was made appealing in your hearts” shows how a fearful assumption became attractive. It justified self-protection and let them feel wiser than obedient believers. Sin often gains power by being decorated as realism, responsibility, or insight rather than appearing ugly. Satan and repeated desire beautify conclusions the heart already wants.\n\n“You harboured evil thoughts about Allah” identifies the deepest error. They treated His support as unreliable and His Messenger’s obedience as certain ruin. Believers may feel fear and assess risk, but they do not conclude that Allah abandons faithful obedience. Good expectation does not guarantee one imagined outcome; it trusts His wisdom and final promise.\n\n“You became a doomed people” means corrupt expectations and refusal placed them on a road to ruin. This warns without closing repentance to anyone alive. Their story teaches that hidden assumptions matter. A person should test discouraging forecasts against revelation, avoid calling cowardice prudence, and ask why a narrative feels appealing. Hudaybiyyah proved that the heart’s confident forecast can be exactly backward: those expecting death returned with serenity, divine pleasure, and the announcement of clear triumph.\n\nThe phrase “return to their families” also exposes the irony of their excuse. They claimed family concern kept them home, while privately believing the Prophet’s companions would lose their own families forever. True care is not selfish exemption from every shared duty. Families thrive when members trust Allah, fulfill communal obligations according to capacity, and refuse to build security upon others’ sacrifice alone. The verse does not diminish family responsibility; it corrects the use of family as a respectable cover for disbelief in Allah’s care.",
  "13": "“Whoever does not believe in Allah and His Messenger” states the essential issue after excuses are exposed. Faith cannot accept Allah while knowingly rejecting the Messenger He sent. Muhammad’s message is the authorized path by which this community learns worship, guidance, and final warning.\n\nThe general “whoever” crosses tribe, sex, wealth, and location. Nomadic background does not condemn, and closeness to Medina does not save. Each person answers for the response to clear guidance. Sincere uncertainty should receive patient evidence; the verse concerns real nonbelief, whose inward extent Allah knows perfectly.\n\n“We surely have prepared for the disbelievers a blazing Fire” confirms that disbelief carried into death has eternal consequence. “Prepared” gives certainty rather than a threat that might be forgotten. “Blazing” communicates intense punishment and urgently calls listeners away. The warning comes from the Lord who just promised Gardens and forgiveness to believing men and women.\n\nNo believer may use this verse to assume a named living person’s final destination or commit injustice against non-Muslims. Worldly dealings remain governed by justice, covenants, and wise invitation. Final judgment belongs to Allah, who knows what message reached each person and how it was answered. For the reader, safety lies in renewing faith in both Allah and His Messenger and refusing to hide rejection beneath acceptable excuses. The Fire is mentioned while escape through sincere faith and repentance remains possible.\n\nBelief in the Messenger includes accepting the Quran he delivered, his final prophethood, and his established explanation of worship. It is more than acknowledging that a historical teacher existed. At the same time, this belief is never worship of Muhammad; he remained Allah’s servant and messenger. The two objects of faith in the opening phrase establish this distinction. Allah is believed in as Lord and God, while Muhammad is believed in as the truthful human envoy whose guidance must be followed because the Lord authorized it.",
  "14": "“To Allah alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth” answers anyone imagining safety, punishment, or reward outside His control. His kingdom includes visible authorities and unseen realities. He owns by creation and sovereign right, while human ownership is temporary, limited, and accountable.\n\n“He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills” places final judgment with Him, exercised through perfect justice and knowledge. His will is not arbitrary. Revelation explains that sincere repentance is accepted and that dying upon unrepented association with Him is not forgiven. Countless individual details are known only to Him.\n\n“Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” ends with hope after warning. The order invites those who stayed behind and lied to repent instead of despairing. “All-Forgiving” indicates vast and repeated forgiveness; “Most Merciful” means mercy belongs perfectly to Him. Punishment is deserved justice, while forgiveness is generous grace.\n\nThe verse prevents opposite errors. No person can guarantee forgiveness while defiantly persisting, and no person can close a door Allah leaves open. His ownership means no institution or imagined intercessor can overrule Him, but His names encourage return. Truthful confession, corrected faith, and repaired conduct are better than another excuse. Believers remain humble because hope does not rest on controlling the verdict. It rests on serving the King whose mercy exceeds their sins and whose judgment cannot be manipulated.\n\nFor those addressed after Hudaybiyyah, the names were a direct invitation. Their secret fear and lies were serious, but they had not yet died upon them. The King who could punish also described Himself as forgiving and merciful. A person should never use divine decree as an excuse to remain unchanged, saying forgiveness belongs only to whomever Allah wills. Revelation shows that willing repentance is itself a road He loves and commands. The servant moves toward mercy while leaving the final judgment humbly with its Owner.",
  "15": "“Those who stayed behind will say, when you believers set out to take the spoils of war, ‘Let us accompany you.’” Danger has passed, and gain now makes participation attractive. This change is not repentance; they want the reward of obedience they refused when obedience looked costly.\n\n“They wish to change Allah’s promise” refers to gains reserved for those who accompanied the Prophet at Hudaybiyyah, especially in relation to Khaybar. Allowing opportunists to join would alter the announced allocation. “You will not accompany us. This is what Allah has said before” makes exclusion revelation, not personal revenge.\n\n“You are driven by jealousy against us!” is their counteraccusation. Instead of examining earlier distrust, they reinterpret obedience as envy. A resistant heart often assigns an ugly motive to a person enforcing a rightful boundary. “They can hardly comprehend” means they grasp little of Allah’s wisdom and the moral cause of the decision.\n\nThe verse distinguishes forgiveness from entitlement. A repentant person can return to Allah without demanding every missed worldly opportunity. Consequences may remain after forgiveness, and rewards tied to earlier sacrifice need not pass to those who avoided it. Leaders cannot use this passage for favoritism; this restriction came from Allah. The broader lesson is to serve before profit appears, receive correction without invented accusations, and accept that sincere return seeks Allah’s pleasure rather than immediate access to benefits.\n\nTheir accusation of jealousy also illustrates projection. They themselves were moved by desire for wealth, then imagined the believers’ refusal must also arise from a worldly passion. Revelation supplies the missing moral category: people may deny a profitable request simply because Allah said no. Understanding faith requires believing that obedience can overrule material interest. When this category is absent, sincerity is continually translated into rivalry. The remedy is learning Allah’s promise, acknowledging one’s prior choice, and allowing moral truth rather than wounded pride to explain the boundary.",
  "16": "“You will be called to fight against a people of great might” gives those who stayed behind another future test. Exclusion from immediate spoils is not permanent exclusion from obedience. They will face a formidable opponent, allowing their claim to be proved where sacrifice is again real. Commentators differed over the historical people intended, but the moral test is clear without insisting upon one identification.\n\n“You will fight unless they submit” concerns a lawful campaign under legitimate Muslim authority, never private violence. Submission here is interpreted within historical and legal context. The statement cannot be detached from Quranic rules governing treaties, noncombatants, and justice.\n\n“If you then obey, Allah will grant you a fine reward.” Past failure does not remove every road to good. Obedience in the next test can demonstrate change and bring reward beyond worldly gains. “If you turn away as you did before,” repeated refusal exposes the excuse and brings “a painful punishment.”\n\nAccountability is therefore joined with another opportunity. The verse neither pretends the first betrayal was harmless nor defines people forever by it. Genuine repentance accepts the next duty even when easy profit is absent. Modern readers leave armed conflict to lawful authority and qualified judgment. The personal principle remains: when a new opportunity to obey follows an old failure, meet it honestly rather than repeating the pattern that words once concealed.\n\n“A fine reward” is deliberately broader than spoils. If they obey sincerely, the most important gain is Allah’s acceptance, whether or not worldly provision accompanies it. This corrects the motive exposed in the preceding verse. They asked to join when spoils were near; they are now called toward a duty where struggle is near. The contrast gives repentance a practical shape. Real change serves when the outcome is uncertain, whereas opportunism appears only when profit can already be counted. Allah knows which response comes from the heart.",
  "17": "“There is no blame on the blind, or the disabled, or the sick for staying behind” establishes clear exemptions from military participation. Islam does not measure faith by forcing people into duties beyond physical capacity. Blindness, mobility limitation, and illness can make combat impossible or seriously harmful, so Allah removes blame.\n\nThe exemption is legal and compassionate, not a claim that disabled people lack value. They may excel in knowledge, counsel, worship, work, generosity, and other service. Individuals differ, so ability for one contribution need not remove exemption from another. Communities should provide dignity and access rather than discussing disabled people only through absence.\n\n“Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger will be admitted by Him into Gardens under which rivers flow.” Reward is opened to everyone through obedience suited to their circumstances. Paradise is not reserved for physical strength. Allah admits the servant, and flowing rivers express lasting life and provision.\n\n“Whoever turns away will be subjected by Him to a painful punishment.” Turning away is deliberate refusal, not inability Allah explicitly excused. This protects vulnerable people from shame while preserving responsibility for the able. No leader should manipulate illness into guilt, and no able person should imitate disability as an excuse. Allah knows capacity perfectly. The verse teaches compassionate law: duties are real, exemptions are real, and both belong to obedience. A faithful community enables each member to serve within ability and never confuses bodily limitation with spiritual failure.\n\nThe verse also challenges communities to distinguish exemption from exclusion. Someone excused from battle remains fully part of the believers and shares the central invitation to Allah and His Messenger. The early Muslim community included disabled Companions whose contributions and dignity were recognized. Accessibility today follows the same moral direction: teaching, mosques, communication, and service should be arranged so bodily differences do not create needless barriers. Mercy in law should become mercy in communal design, while personal obedience flourishes through whatever sound abilities Allah has given.",
  "18": "“Indeed, Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you ˹O Prophet˺ under the tree. He knew what was in their hearts, so He sent down serenity upon them and rewarded them with a victory at hand,” is the verse that gave the pledge of Hudaybiyyah its immortal name, for the commentators tell us that this pledge came to be called the Pledge of Ridwan — the Pledge of Good Pleasure — from the opening words of this very verse. “Indeed, Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you O Prophet under the tree,” or “laqad radiya Allahu 'ani al-mu'mineena idh yubayi'oonaka tahta ash-shajarah.” Ibn Kathir relates the occasion with the detail the verse deserves: when the Prophet had sent Uthman into Mecca as his envoy and the rumor spread through the camp that Uthman had been killed, the Prophet called the companions to pledge themselves to stand firm, and they came to him and placed their hands in his hand beneath a tree at Hudaybiyyah, pledging to fight and not to flee, and God declared from above the seven heavens that He was pleased with them. The number of those who pledged is recorded as about fourteen hundred, and the verse tells us that their pledge was received by God with pleasure, a word that no later event could revoke.\n\nAl-Tabari observes the weight of the phrase “Allah was pleased.” The pleasure of God is the highest station a soul can reach, for the Garden itself is described in the Quran as the place where the believers will have what they desire and “greater still” — the good pleasure of God — and the verse confers upon the companions of the tree the assurance of that pleasure while they were still alive, a gift whose like the believers after them could only hope for. The verse then explains the ground of the pleasure: “He knew what was in their hearts,” or “fa-'alima ma fee quloobihim,” for their pledge was not a gesture of the hand but a resolve of the heart, and God, who knows the hearts, saw in them the sincerity and the readiness to die that made the pledge worthy of His pleasure. Al-Razi notes that the knowledge of the hearts is emphasized because a pledge can be given with the hand while the heart is elsewhere, and the companions' pledge was of the heart, and it was the heart that God accepted.\n\nThe verse then records the two rewards: “so He sent down serenity upon them and rewarded them with a victory at hand,” or “fa-anzala as-sakeenata 'alayhim wa athabahum fathan qareeba.” The serenity is the peace that stilled their fear in the moment of the pledge, and the “victory at hand” is the conquest of Khaybar, which followed soon after, or the treaty itself and its fruits, for the commentators mention both. Al-Qurtubi notes that the verse is a promise to every believer who pledges to God with a sincere heart, that the sincerity of the heart brings the serenity of the spirit and the victory of the cause, and the believer is taught to make his own pledges pledges of the heart, for the pledge of the hand alone reaches no further than the hand.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the honor of the companions of the tree and the reason for that honor, and is taught to seek the pleasure of God through the sincerity that the verse names, for the pleasure of God is not bought with the outward deed but with the inward truth that God alone sees. The verse stands as a window into the moment when a small band of believers, cut off from their homes and facing a mightier enemy, placed their hands in the hand of their Prophet and were told that the Lord of the worlds was pleased with them.",
  "19": "“Many spoils of war they will gain” continues the near reward promised to the believers of the pledge. Material gain did not create their sincerity; they pledged when danger, not wealth, stood before them. Because they proved faithful without visible profit, Allah later granted lawful provision.\n\nCommentators connect these spoils especially with Khaybar and later openings. Islamic law regulates such gains through authority and distribution; this is no permission for individuals to seize property or call theft a religious prize. What became lawful did so through revelation and rules governing a recognized campaign.\n\n“Allah is Almighty” assures that fortresses and opposing forces cannot prevent provision He decrees. “All-Wise” means gains come with right timing and allocation. He withheld immediate entry into Mecca yet opened another provision, teaching that denied expectations do not equal denied care.\n\nThe short verse also tests motives after success. Believers receive wealth while remembering serenity, pledge, and obedience came first. Spoils should create gratitude, not attachment that corrupts the cause. Modern Muslims read this as established sacred history, not a program for unauthorized war. Its broader lesson is that Allah may reward sacrifice materially and spiritually, lawful wealth is not shameful, and every gain remains subject to His commands. Provision is safest when received as entrusted reward rather than proof of superiority.\n\nBecause the gains were “many,” the promise also displays abundance after a season of apparent restriction. The pilgrims had been blocked from Mecca and returned without completing the intended pilgrimage, but their Lord was not withholding all good. One closed route did not empty His provision. This helps believers avoid interpreting a particular denial as total abandonment. They may grieve the closed door while remaining alert to lawful doors opening elsewhere. Wisdom lies in receiving the new provision gratefully without pretending the original disappointment was unreal.",
  "20": "“Allah has promised you believers abundant spoils, which you will gain” widens the horizon beyond one event. The community would receive further lawful gains as opposition receded. The promise came while circumstances looked uncertain, teaching reliance on Allah’s knowledge of the future.\n\n“He hastened this truce for you” identifies Hudaybiyyah as an immediate gift. Though some Companions found its terms painful, peace protected lives, reduced hostility, and opened space for the message. The translation makes the near gift the truce rather than only material spoils.\n\n“He has held people’s hands back from harming you” includes protection from enemies who might attack the pilgrims or threaten Medina in their absence. Restraint can be as real a victory as defeating an attacker. Allah controls hands by known and unseen means, while believers still guard responsibly.\n\n“So it may be a sign for the believers” means fulfilled protection strengthens faith in later promises. “So He may guide you along the Straight Path” shows events can educate. The straight path includes trusting Prophetic judgment, recognizing peace as help, and using gains without losing moral direction. Success is not merely receiving resources; it is remaining guided through them. The verse trains believers to notice prevented harms, not only visible gifts. A danger that never reached them may be one of Allah’s greatest favors, although it leaves no dramatic story.\n\nGuidance “along the Straight Path” after protection prevents a common error: people may seek Allah desperately during danger and then wander once safe. The sign is meant to deepen obedience, not merely provide relief. The treaty created new possibilities for teaching, travel, and interaction; those possibilities needed moral direction. Every protected opportunity raises a question about its use. If peace only produces heedlessness, its lesson is missed. If it produces clearer faith, wider invitation, and grateful service, the worldly protection has become guidance toward eternal good.",
  "21": "“There are other gains which are beyond your reach” tells believers that present ability does not define future possibility. Victories or benefits lay outside what they could then imagine or achieve. Human planning sees current resources; revelation announces that Allah’s store is larger.\n\n“Allah is keeping in store for you” means He has encompassed and reserved those gains through knowledge and decree. What servants cannot reach is easy for their Lord. Commentators connected this with later conquests and differed over specific territories. The wording can embrace broad openings without forcing only one identification.\n\n“Allah is Most Capable of everything” supplies the foundation. His power does not depend upon present numbers, equipment, or political reach. Yet promise did not cancel preparation. The Prophet continued organizing, negotiating, teaching, and acting; divine ability worked through obedient means.\n\nThis offers hope without authorizing wishful claims about every project. This was revelation to Muhammad and his community in a defined setting. Believers today know Allah can open what seems closed, but do not know that a particular worldly gain has been promised. They ask, plan lawfully, and accept His choice. The most important stored gains may be rewards no eye presently sees. Awareness of complete divine ability prevents despair, while humility about His specific decree prevents presumption. Faith works between those two protections.\n\nBeing “beyond your reach” can describe a present fact without defining permanent destiny. This language encourages realistic assessment: the believers truly could not obtain those gains then. Faith did not require pretending otherwise. Hope entered by remembering whose power surrounds the limitation. A person may therefore say, “I cannot currently reach this,” without saying, “Allah can never open it.” Such speech joins honesty about weakness with confidence in divine ability. It always supports careful effort and protects against both fantasy and despair.",
  "22": "“If the disbelievers were to fight you” describes conflict that might have erupted around Hudaybiyyah. The believers appeared vulnerable as pilgrims near hostile Mecca, but appearance did not decide the result. Had opponents begun battle there, Allah declares believers would have been supported.\n\n“They would certainly flee” foretells retreat for the aggressors. This is a specific assurance, not a claim that every Muslim army wins regardless of justice, preparation, leadership, or obedience. Elsewhere the Quran records setbacks and their causes. Here Allah reveals what would have happened in this encounter.\n\n“They would never find any protector or helper” means no ultimate ally could shield them from His decree. A protector could attempt defense and a helper offer aid, but none could overcome Allah. Their idols had no power, while political alliances remained created and limited.\n\nThis helps explain why accepting truce was not cowardice. Muhammad chose peace while possessing divine support; restraint came from wisdom, not helplessness. Confidence in Allah does not require seeking combat, and peacemaking does not prove fear. If fighting had been imposed, believers were to stand; when treaty served guidance, they accepted Prophetic judgment. Strength under control is more faithful than aggression used to prove courage. The Lord who can make an enemy flee can also grant victory by holding every hand back.\n\nThe mention of protector and helper echoes the surah’s emphasis that all forces belong to Allah. An enemy may count alliances while overlooking the Lord who governs every ally. Believers should not copy this error from the opposite side by treating political support as a guarantee. They fulfill agreements and value assistance, but the heart depends finally on Allah. If He grants peace, they are grateful; if defense becomes unavoidable, they seek His help through lawful preparation. In both cases, confidence remains disciplined by obedience rather than turned into boastfulness.",
  "23": "“Allah’s way, already long established in the past” places promised support within a repeated divine pattern. Across prophetic history, persistent aggression against clear truth may dominate temporarily but cannot defeat Allah’s purpose. Messengers and sincere followers receive final vindication in the form His wisdom selects.\n\nA divine “way” does not mean every event is identical. Prophets faced varied tests: some communities were destroyed, some believers migrated, and some victories came through patience or treaty. The stable principle is that Allah’s decree and moral order cannot be overturned by hostile power.\n\n“You will find no change in Allah’s way” gives certainty. People cannot pressure Him into abandoning justice or make falsehood permanently victorious over His revealed purpose. This does not let readers predict exact dates, identify every political cause as prophetic truth, or ignore conditions placed on support. Human claims can be mistaken while Allah’s way never is.\n\nHudaybiyyah broadened the believers’ understanding of that way. They might expect support only as immediate entry or battlefield victory, but Allah provided truce, protection, and future opening. The verse creates firmness with intellectual humility. Trust the unchanging Lord, but remain teachable about changing means He selects. Study earlier revelation for patterns, obey within present circumstances, and never turn one preferred strategy into part of what Allah promised never to change.\n\nAn unchanging way also means moral cause and consequence are not random, even when the interval is long. Patience is necessary because people often see only a small segment of the pattern. Earlier messengers endured years before the promised outcome, and some faithful servants died before worldly vindication appeared. Their final vindication is not lost, because the Hereafter completes Allah’s justice. Therefore “no change” should be read across the full horizon revelation provides, not reduced to a guarantee of immediate comfort for every righteous individual.",
  "24": "“He is the One Who held back their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of ˹Ḥudaibiyah, near˺ Mecca, after giving you the upper hand over ˹a group of˺ them. And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.” recalls the restraint that hung over the whole encounter at Hudaybiyyah, and the verse answers the question that every reader of the story asks: why did the two sides, armed and hostile and facing each other, not come to blows? “He is the One Who held back their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Hudaybiyyah, near Mecca, after giving you the upper hand over a group of them,” or “wa huwa alladhi kaffa aydiyahum 'ankum wa aydiyakum 'anhum bi-batni Makkata min ba'di an athfarakum 'alayhim.” Ibn Kathir relates the incident the verse refers to: a band of the Quraysh had come out against the Muslims, and the Muslims took them, and the Prophet released them rather than fight, so that the encounter at the very edge of Mecca ended without blood, and the verse declares that this restraint was not the result of the Muslims' weakness or the Quraysh's mercy but the act of God, who held back the hands of both sides.\n\nAl-Tabari observes the double restraint in the verse — God held back the hands of the enemy from the believers, and held back the hands of the believers from the enemy — and the believer is taught that peace, no less than victory, is the gift of God, and that the hand which is held back from shedding blood is held back by the same power that could have let it strike. The verse also notes that this restraint came “after giving you the upper hand over a group of them,” for the Muslims had actually prevailed over that band, and the restraint was therefore an act of choice and mercy, not of necessity, and the believer is taught that true strength is shown as much in the hand that refrains as in the hand that strikes. Al-Razi notes that the valley of Hudaybiyyah is named as the place, and the mention of Mecca adds the irony and the lesson, for the two sides stood at the very threshold of the Sacred Sanctuary, and God held their hands back from defiling it with blood.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the hand of God in the events that do not happen as much as in the events that do, for the battles that never occurred and the blood that was never shed are among the greatest of God's favors, and the believer is taught to thank God for the calamities He has averted no less than for the blessings He has granted. The verse closes with, “And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do,” or “wa kana Allahu bima ta'maloona baseera,” the reminder that the restraint and the choice were witnessed, and that the believer's own choices are witnessed too. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to practice restraint in the hour of provocation, to know that the hand held back for God's sake is a hand that God holds, and to remember that the peace of Hudaybiyyah, which the Muslims did not choose and did not want, was the peace that God had chosen and from which the great triumph grew.",
  "25": "“They are the ones who persisted in disbelief and hindered you from the Sacred Mosque, preventing the sacrificial animals from reaching their destination. ˹We would have let you march through Mecca,˺ had there not been believing men and women, unknown to you. You might have trampled them underfoot, incurring guilt for ˹what you did to˺ them unknowingly. That was so Allah may admit into His mercy whoever He wills. Had those ˹unknown˺ believers stood apart, We would have certainly inflicted a painful punishment on the disbelievers.” explains the deeper reason for the restraint, and the verse opens a window into a wisdom that no one at Hudaybiyyah could have seen. “They are the ones who persisted in disbelief and hindered you from the Sacred Mosque, preventing the sacrificial animals from reaching their destination,” or “humu allatheena kafaroo wa saddookum 'ani al-Masjidi al-Harami wal-hadya ma'koofan an yablugha mahillahu.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse first states the guilt of Quraysh — they were the ones who disbelieved and who barred the Prophet from the Sacred Mosque and prevented the sacrificial animals from reaching the place of sacrifice — so that no one should think that the restraint was a reward for their good conduct, for their guilt was full and the restraint was mercy, not approval.\n\nThen the verse reveals the hidden reason: “We would have let you march through Mecca, had there not been believing men and women, unknown to you. You might have trampled them underfoot, incurring guilt for what you did to them unknowingly,” or “wa law la rijalun mu'minoona wa nisa'un mu'minatun lam ta'lamoohum an tata'oohum fa-tuseebakum minhum ma'arratun bi-ghayri 'ilmin.” Al-Tabari relates the meaning: there were, within Mecca, men and women who had believed in secret, whose faith was hidden from the Muslims, and had the Muslims fought their way into the city they might have killed these hidden believers without knowing, and the believers would then have borne the guilt of shedding the blood of fellow believers. God held back the fighting to protect those hidden faithful, and the believer is taught the depth of the divine care, for God guarded a small number of secret believers at the cost of postponing the triumph, and the value of a single believer in His sight outweighs the haste of victory.\n\nAl-Razi observes the principle the verse teaches: the believer must weigh the hidden costs of what he seeks, for the victory that tramples the innocent is a defeat, and the conquest that sheds the blood of the faithful is a crime, and the wisdom of God often postpones the apparent good to protect the hidden good. The verse then states the other side: “That was so Allah may admit into His mercy whoever He wills. Had those unknown believers stood apart, We would have certainly inflicted a painful punishment on the disbelievers,” or “li-yudkhila Allahu fee rahmatihi man yasha'u law tazayyaloo la-'adhdhabna allatheena kafaroo minhum 'adhaban aleema.” Al-Qurtubi explains that had the believers been separated out from the disbelievers, the punishment would have fallen on the disbelievers then and there, but the mingling of the two held it back, and the believer is taught the mercy that is woven through the very delay he laments.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to trust the wisdom of God in the events that disappoint him, for the restraint at Hudaybiyyah was the protection of believers the Muslims did not know, and the believer is taught to remember that behind every divine postponement there may stand a mercy he cannot yet see, and that the God who protects the hidden believers is the God who protects him.",
  "26": "“˹Remember˺ when the disbelievers had filled their hearts with pride— the pride of ˹pre-Islamic˺ ignorance—then Allah sent down His serenity upon His Messenger and the believers, inspiring them to uphold the declaration of faith, for they were better entitled and more worthy of it. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of all things.” contrasts the two spirits that faced each other at Hudaybiyyah, and the verse names the disease of the one side and the medicine of the other. “Remember when the disbelievers had filled their hearts with pride — the pride of pre-Islamic ignorance — then Allah sent down His serenity upon His Messenger and the believers, inspiring them to uphold the declaration of faith, for they were better entitled and more worthy of it,” or “idh ja'ala allatheena kafaroo fee quloobihimu al-hamiyyata hamiyyata al-jahiliyyati fa-anzala Allahu sakeenatahu 'ala rasoolihi wa 'ala al-mu'mineena wa alzamahum kalimata at-taqwa wa kanu ahaqqa biha wa ahlaha.” Ibn Kathir explains the word “hamiyyah” — the zeal, the haughty pride — and the phrase “the pride of ignorance,” for it was the pride of the age before Islam, the pride that refused to write in the treaty the words “in the name of Allah, the Most Merciful” and refused to acknowledge Muhammad as the messenger of God, the pride of men whose honor was in their ancestry and their idols rather than in the truth.\n\nAl-Tabari observes that the verse teaches the believer the root of the conflict between truth and falsehood — it is not a conflict of armies but of spirits, the spirit of pride against the spirit of serenity, the “hamiyyah” of ignorance against the “sakeenah” of God — and the believer is taught to watch which spirit fills his own heart. The verse then names the gift given to the believers: “the declaration of faith,” or “kalimat at-taqwa” — the word of God-consciousness — which the commentators identify as the testimony “there is no god but Allah,” the word that the disbelievers' pride had refused to write and that God fixed in the hearts of the believers, and the verse says they were “better entitled and more worthy of it,” for the word of piety belongs to the pious and the pride of ignorance belongs to the ignorant. Al-Razi notes the beauty of the contrast — the disbelievers were filled with their own pride, and the believers were filled with the serenity of God and the word of piety, and the outcome of the two fillings is the whole history that followed.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to empty his heart of the “hamiyyah” of his own age, the pride of tribe and nation and party that masquerades as honor, and to fill it with the serenity and the word of piety, for the pride of ignorance blinds while the word of piety opens the eyes. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to recognize the pride of ignorance in whatever form it appears and to refuse it, to hold fast to the word of God-consciousness that God has given him, and to know that the one who is filled with serenity and piety is the one who is truly entitled to the triumph, while the one who is filled with pride is the one for whom the triumph becomes a judgment.",
  "27": "“Indeed, Allah will fulfil His Messenger’s vision in all truth: Allah willing, you will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, in security—˹some with˺ heads shaved and ˹others with˺ hair shortened—without fear. He knew what you did not know, so He first granted you the triumph at hand.” closes the surah's account of Hudaybiyyah with the promise that gathered up the whole story, and the verse concerns the vision that had set the journey in motion. “Indeed, Allah will fulfil His Messenger's vision in all truth: Allah willing, you will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, in security — some with heads shaved and others with hair shortened — without fear,” or “laqad sadaqa Allahu rasoolahu ar-ru'ya bil-haqqi la-tadkhulunna al-Masjida al-Harama in sha'a Allahu amineena muhalliqeena ru'oosakum wa muqassireena la takhafoon.” Ibn Kathir relates the background: the Prophet had seen in a dream that he and his companions were entering the Sacred Mosque, and it was in fulfillment of that vision that he had set out for Mecca, and when Quraysh turned them back at Hudaybiyyah, some of the companions doubted whether the vision would be fulfilled, and this verse was revealed to declare that the vision was true and would be fulfilled in its time. The dream of a prophet is a form of revelation, and the verse calls the vision “the truth,” or “bil-haqq,” and swears to its fulfillment.\n\nAl-Tabari observes the precision of the promise, for the verse describes the very details that were fulfilled the following year in the Umrah of al-Qada', when the Prophet and his companions entered Mecca in security and performed the pilgrimage, some with their heads shaved and others with their hair shortened, in the full rites of the pilgrim, and the verse had spoken of these details before they happened, so that the fulfillment became a sign of the truth of the Prophet. The phrase “Allah willing,” or “in sha'a Allah,” is noted by the commentators as the mark of the believer's submission, for the promise of God is certain and yet the believer attaches every future to the will of God, and the verse itself teaches the words to say when speaking of what is to come. Al-Razi notes the phrase “He knew what you did not know, so He first granted you the triumph at hand,” or “fa-'alima ma lam ta'lamoo fa-ja'ala min dooni thalika fathan qareeba,” for the delay of the pilgrimage was not a denial of the vision but the wisdom of God, who knew the hidden believers in Mecca and the good that the delay would bring, and who granted in the meantime the victory of Khaybar as a foretaste of what was to come.\n\nAl-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to hold the promises of God with certainty while accepting their timing with submission, for the vision was true and was fulfilled, but in the year that God chose and not the year that the companions wanted, and the believer is taught that the promise of God is never broken and never late, for His timing is the timing of wisdom. Al-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to trust the dreams and promises that come from God, to say “Allah willing” for every future, and to know that the delay of a desired good is not its denial but the preparation of a greater good that God alone can see.",
  "28": "“He is the One Who has sent His Messenger with right guidance” attributes Muhammad’s mission to Allah. Guidance includes true knowledge, sound worship, and the path that brings people from confusion toward their Lord. Muhammad did not design a private spiritual program; he carried revelation authorized by the Creator.\n\n“And the religion of truth” describes Islam as true in belief, law, worship, and final purpose. Its truth is not produced by political success. Rather, its promised prevalence follows from its divine source. “Making it prevail over all others” has been explained as Allah making its proofs and truth superior and granting historical ascendancy, with complete manifestation according to His promise.\n\n“And sufficient is Allah as a Witness” removes dependence on hostile recognition. Allah witnesses that He sent Muhammad and that the message is true. His testimony appears through revelation, signs, fulfilled promises, and the Quran’s enduring proof. No human denial can cancel it.\n\nPrevalence does not license forced conversion or injustice. Faith cannot be made sincere through compulsion, and Islamic dominance remains bound to Prophetic mercy, covenants, and justice. The verse came amid a treaty, showing that truth can advance through peaceful contact as well as political opening. Believers support its prevalence first by learning and living it accurately, not by attaching its name to conduct that contradicts its guidance.\n\n“The religion of truth” also sets a standard for those representing it. Lies, oppression, and superstition do not become true because a Muslim employs them. The religion prevails by its own divine evidence, while its followers are honored when their conduct reflects that evidence. If they fail, their failure does not make revelation false, but it may hide its beauty from others and bring accountability. Confidence in Allah’s witness should therefore create integrity, not laziness. He witnesses both the message’s truth and what its professed followers do with it.",
  "29": "“Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah” states his identity and authority plainly. He is neither divine nor merely an admired reformer. “Those with him are firm with the disbelievers and compassionate with one another.” Firmness means principled strength against hostile rejection, not cruelty toward every non-Muslim. Compassion governs believers’ mutual relationships and should appear in support, forgiveness, and sincere counsel.\n\n“You see them bowing and prostrating in prayer, seeking Allah’s bounty and pleasure.” Their public strength grows from humble worship. They seek provision and grace, but above all divine acceptance. “The sign of brightness can be seen on their faces from the trace of prostrating” refers to the light, humility, and effect of worship, not necessarily a physical mark that proves righteousness.\n\n“This is their description in the Torah.” Their qualities were known in earlier revelation. The Gospel parable compares them to “a seed that sprouts its tiny branches,” then grows strong, thick, and firm, delighting planters. The small persecuted community developed under Prophetic cultivation into a stable people.\n\nAllah thereby makes believers “a source of dismay for the disbelievers” who hate guidance, because healthy growth frustrates their wish for its disappearance. This is not an invitation to provoke people through bad character. “To those of them who believe and do good,” Allah promises forgiveness and great reward. The ending prevents relying on mere companionship or label; saving promise belongs to persevering faith and good action.\n\nThe seed image also teaches gradual development. A branch begins tiny, receives nourishment, strengthens alongside others, and eventually helps the whole planting stand. New believers and young communities need cultivation rather than contempt for being small. Growth becomes delightful when rooted, balanced, and mutually supporting. Firmness without compassion makes the plant harsh; compassion without principle leaves it unable to stand. Bowing and prostration nourish both qualities. The final promise directs admiration away from personality worship and toward the faith and good deeds Allah accepts."
};
